course: dictionary
lesson: 9446
title: Stamina: keeping going
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Stamina is the strength and energy to keep doing something hard for a long time without getting tired. Think of a long-distance runner or a swimmer doing length after length. Stamina is about how long you can keep going, not how fast or how hard you can push.
explain: Context helps you spot this meaning. «Strength (how hard a muscle can push) and stamina (how long you can keep going) are different.» The brackets set the two meanings side by side, so you can see that stamina is the one about time.
explain: Stamina is built, not just given. «Stamina is built by things like running, swimming or cycling.» Keep moving regularly and it grows; stop, and it fades. You can also need stamina for mental effort, like finishing a big project.
fact: Stamina is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some stamina, not a stamina.
fact: «Stamina» means the strength and energy to keep doing something hard for a long time without getting tired.
fact: Strength is how hard a muscle can push; stamina is how long you can keep going. They are different.
fact: Stamina is built by regular running, swimming and cycling.
fact: Useful phrases: build up stamina, have the stamina to, lack stamina.
exercise: «It takes stamina to finish a big project.» What does stamina mean here? | A kind of healthy food | The strength to lift something heavy | *The energy to keep going for a long time | A quick burst of speed
exercise: Ella wants more stamina for her cross-country race. Which activity will help her most? | *Steady long-distance running | Waiting for the race to start | Sitting still and reading | Lifting one heavy weight, once
exercise: «Strength and stamina are different.» Find and copy the word that means how hard a muscle can push. | Stamina | Keep | Holidays | *Strength
test: Which phrase means having too little energy to keep going? | *Lack stamina | Have the stamina to | Build up stamina
test: «Grey wolves hunt over long distances; they are built for stamina rather than speed.» What are wolves built for? | Speed alone | Hiding in trees | *Stamina rather than speed | Sleeping all day
exam: Ben says, «Strength and stamina mean the same thing, so I can use either word.» What is the right answer? | Yes, they mean exactly the same | *No, strength is how hard a muscle can push, and stamina is how long you can keep going | Yes, but only in PE lessons | No, stamina is only for mental effort
